ny interesting um it's such a  fertile ground for this  a jackson pollock painting isn't another  thing because he's organized it somewhat  aesthetically on his own structures  we're getting a different kind of  aesthetics from a jackson hollow  painting then we will from a  field of wildflowers or a spider web or  any or the  structure of the universe for example  can i uh pick up on that a little bit  just for a minute um so i also  some one of the prior questions was  asking if people have  have sonified maps of the universe  and i i guess the answer without knowing  specifically who  is yes because people are sonifying  everything but one thing i want to say  about sonification  whether it's of the natural world or of  a jackson pollock is that  you cannot leave out the amount of human  choice that goes into  sonifying and this is something i  mentioned in talking about  earlier sonifications uh and  i i was thinking about in particular  because another colleague of ian's in  mine  jesse taylor who's in physics actually  sent me recently a sonification of the  i will mispronounce this and i have no  idea what it is the  daimuan invariant spectrum  and uh you know was really excited and  sent me  all the vibrations of of uh  you know edda omega and phi and epsilon  1s and upsilon 2s  etc and uh and various diagrams  and and then various sonifications all  of which sounded very different because  as soon as you sonify all these  frequencies you just go  wait what now what do i do with this and  i think  the the way that one has to look at this  is that in the same way that if anybody  has tried to make a  chart or a diagram or a data  visualization  you know that even if you know very well  what it is that you're trying to  visualize  you have to really put the psychology of  yourself  and your audience into your mind if  you're going to make an effective  visualization  and the same is true with sonification  it's really just not a it's not a matter  of just goin